          %h2#Introduction Survey
          %p
            We are concerned with the theory of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            in the sense of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %h3#StableHomotopyTheorySurvey 1) Stable homotopy theory
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            in homotopy theory is equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
            under concatenation of loops (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group"}> ∞-group
            ”). A double loop space is a group with some commutativity structure (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+argument"}> Eckmann-Hilton argument
            ”), a triple loop space has more commutativity structure, and so forth. A
            %em spectrum
            is where this progression of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/looping+and+delooping"} looping and delooping
            %em stabilizes
            (an “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-abelian group”). Therefore one speaks of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spaces
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munderover
                    %mo ↦
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi linearization
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi stabilization
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Spectra
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Spaces
                  \\;\;
                  \\underoverset{(linearization)}{stabilization}{\mapsto}
                  \\;\;
                  Spectra
                  \\,.
          %p
            Most of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            passes along as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            are generalized to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            and turns into something remarkably rich, called
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brave+new+algebra"} brave new algebra
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+geometry"} spectral geometry
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            exists, given by (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring+spectrum"}> commutative
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+spectra"} ring spectra
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+rings"}> E-∞ rings
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+rings"} A-∞ rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+spectra"} module spectra
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-modules"}> ∞-modules
            ).
          %h3#ANSS 2) Adams spectral sequences
          %p
            Since spectra are considerably richer than abelian groups, stable homotopy is much concerned with “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fracture+theorem"}> fracturing
            ” stable homotopy types into more tractable components:
          %p
            To that end, notice that from the point of view of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaf"} quasicoherent sheaf
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spec%28Z%29"} Spec(Z)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AbelianGroups
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi QCoh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  AbelianGroups \simeq QCoh(Spec(\mathbb{Z}))
                  \\,.
          %p
            This point of view generalizes to homotopy theory and turns out to be very fruitful there. The analog of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕊
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} initial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring+spectrum"} commutative ring spectrum
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"}> E-∞ ring
            ”), just as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dual"} formal dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spec%28S%29"} Spec(S)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
            is hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} terminal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+arithmetic+geometry"} E-∞ arithmetic geometry
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+geometry"}> spectral geometry
            ”) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            are equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} quasicoherent ∞-stacks
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(\mathbb{S})
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spectra
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi QCoh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Spectra \simeq QCoh(Spec(\mathbb{S}))
                  \\,.
          %p
            Therefore the study of spectra “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fracture+theorem"}> fractures
            ” into the various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizations"} localizations
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+completions"} formal completions
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕊
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(\mathbb{S})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(\mathbb{S})
            decomposing into various wavelengths, one speaks of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+homotopy+theory"} chromatic homotopy theory
          %p
            In particular, an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dual"} dually
            a morphism of
            = succeed "-algebraic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(E) \longrightarrow Spec(\mathbb{S})
            and under good conditions the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-image"} 1-image
            of this map is the formal dual of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization+of+spectra"} localization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi E
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_E \mathbb{S}
            at
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi epi
                        %mn 1
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi E
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi mono
                        %mn 1
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Spec(E) \stackrel{epi_1}{\longrightarrow} Spec(L_E \mathbb{S}) \stackrel{mono_1}{\longrightarrow} Spec(\mathbb{S})
                  \\,.
          %p
            This means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi E
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(E) \longrightarrow Spec(L_E \mathbb{S})
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            and that hence
            = succeed "-local" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} quasicoherent ∞-stacks
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(E)
            equipped with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent+data"} descent data
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dual"} dually
            they are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-modules"} ∞-modules
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comodule"} comodule
            structure over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebroid"} Hopf algebroid
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sweedler+coring"}> Sweedler coring
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi 𝕊
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \otimes_{\mathbb{S}} E
          %p
            The computation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of spectra that make use of their decomposition this way into
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-modules"} ∞-modules
            equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            data is the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+spectral+sequence"} Adams spectral sequence
          %h3#s_complex_oriented_cohomology S) Complex oriented cohomology
          %p
            For this reason special importance is carried by those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+rings"} E-∞ rings
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(E) \to Spec(\mathbb{S})
            is already a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering"} covering
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-modules"} ∞-modules
            equipped with descent data give an equivalent, but in general more tractable, incarnation of the stable homotopy theory of spectra.
          %p
            Curiously, this way a good bit of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+topology"} differential topology
            –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+theory"} cobordism theory
            – arises within stable homotopy theory: the archetypical
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(E)
            which covers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(\mathbb{S})
            in a suitable sense is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E =
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MU"} MU
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+spectrum"} Thom spectrum
            representing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism+cohomology"} complex cobordism cohomology
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring+spectrum"} commutative ring spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi MU
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MU
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi MU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(E)\to Spec(MU)
            is now a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+cohomology+theory"} multiplicative
            “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theory"}> complex oriented cohomology theory
            ”.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %hr/
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h2#ClassicalHomotopyTheory
            %strong Prelude) Classical homotopy theory
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            This section is at:
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P"} Introduction to Stable homotopy theory – P
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h2#StableHomotopyTheory
            %strong Part 1) Stable homotopy theory
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            This section is at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+1"} Introduction to Stable homotopy theory – 1
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
